      "headline": "Blue & You Foundation awards grants for Arkansas health",
      "subhead": "Five nonprofits received surprise awards as part of a million dollar grant cycle for community health initiatives.",
      "sponsor_line": "Sponsored content from Arkansas Blue Cross and Blue Shield",
      "body": "Justin Buck, executive director of the Wolfe Street Foundation, is preparing to open a new support center in Little Rock. \"We're excited to launch the state's first community-based youth recovery program right here in Little Rock,\" Buck said. The Wolfe Street Foundation says the Youth Empowerment Project will be the first program of its kind in Arkansas. It will offer peer support for youth in grades 7-12 to address substance use disorder.\n\nThis project is one of several initiatives supported by the Blue & You Foundation for a Healthier Arkansas. In October, the Foundation surprised five nonprofit organizations with special $25,000 gifts. These recipients also received the Foundation's Innovation in Action awards. The Foundation awarded more than $1 million in grants to more than a dozen programs across Arkansas recently. Organizations were eligible for up to $200,000 each, and mini-grants of up to $2,000 are available on a rolling basis.\n\nThe Arkansas Coalition for Obesity Prevention received $192,600 for its Double Up Food Bucks program, which provides access to locally grown produce. The Northwest Arkansas Food Bank received $25,000 to provide 1.2 million pounds of groceries through a network of 20 mobile pantries. Additionally, the University District Development Corporation will use $200,000 to construct six affordable single-family homes and 10 duplexes in Little Rock.\n\nThe Foundation focuses other funding on mental health and substance use. The Blue & You Foundation announced grants totaling $5.9 million to address these challenges among Arkansas youth. The University of Arkansas for Medical Sciences received $1,610,477 to fund its Pathways to Wellness program and $714,809 for an addiction treatment program. These grants are part of a commitment to improve behavioral health initially announced in 2019.\n\nRebecca Pittillo, president of the Blue & You Foundation, said the awards recognize organizations making a meaningful difference in the lives of Arkansans. \"As we celebrate this milestone, we want to recognize organizations that are making a meaningful difference in the lives of Arkansans every day. These awards are our way of saying 'Thank you' for their partnership, dedication and impact,\" Pittillo said.",
