      "headline": "Blue & You Foundation gifts support healthier Arkansas communities",
      "subhead": "New funding from the Blue & You Foundation helps local organizations address mental health, food access and affordable housing.",
      "sponsor_line": "Sponsored content from Arkansas Blue Cross and Blue Shield",
      "body": "Five nonprofit organizations in Arkansas received a surprise $25,000 gift in October. The Blue & You Foundation for a Healthier Arkansas presented these gifts as part of its Innovation in Action awards. The awards honor organizations that partner with the Foundation to improve health for all Arkansans. \"Twenty-five years ago, the Blue & You Foundation was established with a vision of building healthier communities across Arkansas,\" said Rebecca Pittillo, president of the Blue & You Foundation.\n\nThe Foundation was established in 2001 with an initial gift from Arkansas Blue Cross and Blue Shield. It has since invested more than $70 million in health initiatives across all 75 Arkansas counties. A recent grant cycle awarded more than $1 million to programs addressing needs like housing and food access. These programs are designed to address various social determinants of health.\n\nThe Wolfe Street Foundation is using these resources to help young people in recovery. On July 12, the organization hosted a ribbon cutting for its new Youth Empowerment Project and center in Little Rock. Executive Director Justin Buck said the project helps young Arkansans envision a bright future without the use of drugs or alcohol. The Wolfe Street Foundation says the center will be the state's first community-based youth recovery program.\n\nOther projects are expanding their reach to serve more residents. Restore Hope will use $200,000 to expand its 100 Families Initiative into Mississippi, Independence and Jefferson counties. Hispanic Community Services received $118,795 to provide bilingual education for low-income students in Northeast Arkansas. In Central Arkansas, City Connections will distribute 100,000 meals to approximately 16,700 residents through the Arkansas Hunger Relief Army.\n\nThe Foundation and Arkansas Blue Cross and Blue Shield committed to improving mental health in Arkansas communities three years ago. Pittillo said substance use and mental health challenges are not isolated to certain parts of the state. \"To ensure the well-being of our children, our families and our state, care and resources must be accessible in every corner of Arkansas, to every family, and that's our goal,\" Pittillo said.",
